{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 849 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0455562de1c877facf716499638d6dc4b31e2e3122d132d554aef1b330487dde" :edb/db-before-t 848 :edb/db-after-t 849 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534161 50 #inst "2026-10-06T06:19:54.764Z" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063912 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right)$." 13194139534161 false] [17592186063912 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063912 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{4 + 5x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= - (22)/((4 + 5x)^{2}) \\\\\nf^{′}(-1) &= -22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(-5) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(-5))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(-1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-22)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{22}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 false] [17592186063912 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063913 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063913 1100 "The figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x)$. The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x)$.\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x)$. If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$, what is $(f^{-1})'(0)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/291/Source/Images/d6a83b3149c17f55744d206f77f51649.png)" 13194139534161 false] [17592186063913 1145 "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1}$, we have\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(0) = 2\n$$\n\nWe're also told that\n\n$$\nf'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(x)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(0) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(0))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(2)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 false] [17592186063913 1145 "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063914 1100 "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x)$, and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" 13194139534161 false] [17592186063914 1100 "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063914 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1)\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(1)=2\n$$\n\n$g(1)=-3$, $g'(1)=1$, and $f'(2)=-1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1} \\cdot g)^{′}(1) &= (g(1))/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(1))) + g^{′}(1)f^{-1}(1) \\\\\n&= (-3)/(f^{′}(2)) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3}{-1} + 2 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 false] [17592186063914 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063915 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2)$." 13194139534161 false] [17592186063915 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186063915 1145 "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2)$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2)$. To do this, we solve the equation\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(2) = x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(2) &= x \\\\\nf(x) &= 2 \\\\\n3e^{x} - 1 &= 2 \\\\\n3e^{x} &= 3 \\\\\ne^{x} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(2) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3e^{x} - 1 \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 3e^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 3e^{0} \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(2) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(2))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 false] [17592186063915 1145 "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279528 13194139534161 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279535 13194139534161 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279542 13194139534161 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279587 13194139534161 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279591 13194139534161 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279563 13194139534161 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279570 13194139534161 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279594 13194139534161 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279598 13194139534161 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279601 13194139534161 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279549 13194139534161 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279577 13194139534161 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279604 13194139534161 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279611 13194139534161 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279618 13194139534161 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279556 13194139534161 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279580 13194139534161 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279583 13194139534161 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279608 13194139534161 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279625 13194139534161 true] [17592186279528 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279529 13194139534161 true] [17592186279528 1089 "q-110127" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279528 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534161 true] [17592186279528 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/e09f5a90c683876f849577cd75af3c77cc21c6f9af10c5a4ece47718aa33f738.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linea