lope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2]$ is $-2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279544 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279545 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279546 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279547 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279548 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1041 "selection" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279545 13194139534161 true] [17592186279543 1110 #uuid "068b4da3-a2e0-5ae7-a4bd-7cb5bd0c479f" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279544 1034 "-\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279544 1111 #uuid "fc9bf329-1eed-55c7-a672-f78fbd363769" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279545 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279545 1111 #uuid "014896cb-38e4-55f7-835b-62f9fbf455dd" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279546 1034 "2" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279546 1111 #uuid "7fd6599b-4203-5576-ab15-1332d294ab7f" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279547 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279547 1111 #uuid "b55bcc0f-a090-5ff4-929c-931a4f7d99c1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279548 1034 "-2" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279548 1111 #uuid "f9a28e06-914c-5135-870a-9a7021354620" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279549 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279550 13194139534161 true] [17592186279549 1089 "q-110272" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279549 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534161 true] [17592186279549 1100 "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279549 1113 #uuid "75bdddd1-2f9e-5cb2-9924-d4847b6838c0" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279549 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)=\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=3,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4) & =\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(3)}+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2}+15 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279551 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279552 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279553 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279554 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279555 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1041 "selection" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279551 13194139534161 true] [17592186279550 1110 #uuid "a46c9094-ba55-5f98-a3f1-1d3c3bedbc27" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279551 1034 "\\frac{31}{2}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279551 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279551 1111 #uuid "8cc2dbe6-2683-5c50-9b19-63fd9174cf06" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279552 1034 "15" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279552 1111 #uuid "242b6e9e-1054-54f8-884f-faec3ec6194c" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279553 1034 "\\frac{46}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279553 1111 #uuid "eca4998f-0622-5cfb-8cc2-b8c825273700" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279554 1034 "\\frac{15}{2}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279554 1111 #uuid "75dbe5e6-9df1-5920-a28a-bcb0687ff554" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279555 1034 "\\frac{28}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279555 1111 #uuid "88412c1d-bd9b-5346-aa51-252c52f9ee28" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279556 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279557 13194139534161 true] [17592186279556 1089 "q-110341" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279556 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534161 true] [17592186279556 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+5x+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279556 1113 #uuid "d198f76c-2fe3-50e7-9351-8eafd2f01101" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279556 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+5x+2 & =2 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+5x+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279558 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279559 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279560 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279561 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279562 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1041 "selection" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279561 13194139534161 true] [17592186279557 1110 #uuid "126cafa6-7156-51f1-b07b-bb36ae50f873" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279558 1034 "-5" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279558 1111 #uuid "58514938-c1e9-5d63-8917-72463ffc6022" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279559 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279559 1111 #uuid "1454d265-817d-5f47-9c10-7639b426a74d" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279560 1034 "1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279560 1111 #uuid "a39e44b5-0bd0-593a-8fa1-67c1db7249ae" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279561 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279561 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279561 1111 #uuid "bae59a1b-3263-561a-9cd4-4449ef437b31" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279562 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279562 1111 #uuid "a2aaf225-8d73-5350-a42a-e69aa296da51" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279564 13194139534161 true] [17592186279563 1089 "q-15651" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534161 true] [17592186279563 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$ and the point $P(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{4},7).$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279563 1113 #uuid "9defc19b-b540-5a93-90de-15be5571aa37" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279563 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(7,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{4(x-3)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(7) & =-\\frac{3}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3}{4},7)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{32})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279565 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279566 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279567 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279568 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279569 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1041 "selection" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279565 13194139534161 true] [17592186279564 1110 #uuid "65c78d50-f7fb-52d7-96cb-16cbbe422d3b" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279565 1034 "-\\frac{32}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279565 1111 #uuid "2214a2ef-dfcd-59d9-954f-6871162ceb57" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279566 1034 "\\frac{32}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279566 1111 #uuid "5a6f4abc-bea0-51c9-8e2d-5e031ab0c129" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279567 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279567 1111 #uuid "3df97db9-3edc-5e56-9da4-33f6081aac5f" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279568 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279568 1111 #uuid "08cc8d48-106c-514d-9cb5-2fa415f7034d" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279569 1034 "-\\frac{3}{32}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279569 1111 #uuid "0d8aceb1-18dd-5a1d-9de1-cc181dfa7657" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279570 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279571 13194139534161 true] [17592186279570 1089 "q-15652" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279570 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534161 true] [17592186279570 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x}$ and the point $P(1,-7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-7,1).$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279570 1113 #uuid "71816a61-baa5-5230-bd29-159dd6440cd5" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279570 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,-7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{17}{(2-3x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =17\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279572 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279573 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279574 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279575 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279576 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1041 "selection" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279576 13194139534161 true] [17592186279571 1110 #uuid "cc98d4c2-2902-5abf-9024-1bb5676fba2f" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279572 1034 "-7" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279572 1111 #uuid "66c87dd3-1c92-52cb-92c3-5a09eb773c49" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279573 1034 "-\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279573 1111 #uuid "dc1915c9-6c2b-55c3-b5b0-5df88ed581d5" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279574 1034 "17" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279574 1111 #uuid "2d5bae6b-20ac-538e-891d-8254734ca436" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279575 1034 "1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279575 1111 #uuid "a8edbfcb-1b92-512b-aa38-0c2aafb2eb8b" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279576 1034 "\\frac{1}{17}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279576 1111 #uuid "311e8ecc-4b5d-58be-b482-3bbae10a1e7e" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279577 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279578 13194139534161 true] [17592186279577 1089 "q-312584" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534161 true] [17592186279577 1100 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279577 1113 #uuid "74b2f2fb-bda2-5fab-bba0-63ecfa763a2b" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279577 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=5,$ $g(2)=4,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5+\\frac{4}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =-15+\\frac{4}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-15-4 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279579 13194139534161 true] [17592186279578 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534161 true] [17592186279578 1041 "field-1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279578 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279579 13194139534161 true] [17592186279578 1110 #uuid "63e79ecb-5a2b-5edd-b93c-554db2be15a3" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279579 1034 "-19" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279579 1111 #uuid "ddcd8470-cb6e-52a8-baa6-16cfad6bf91c" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279580 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279581 13194139534161 true] [17592186279580 1089 "q-312588" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279580 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534161 true] [17592186279580 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={4}^{x}+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279580 1113 #uuid "a7e4d2ce-43e6-5f2f-a26a-1291eab1840c" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279580 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(6).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(6)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(6) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x}+2 & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{1} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(6)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={4}^{x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & ={4}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279582 13194139534161 true] [17592186279581 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534161 true] [17592186279581 1041 "field-1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279581 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279582 13194139534161 true] [17592186279581 1110 #uuid "d0ac6665-8270-5b3f-aa0d-3ad34e91de23" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279582 1034 "\\frac{1}{4\\ln(4)}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279582 1111 #uuid "7b8e9a09-eb9e-504b-b42f-8c42ab38cdc8" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279584 13194139534161 true] [17592186279583 1089 "q-312589" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279583 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534161 true] [17592186279583 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279583 1113 #uuid "c939b62d-8700-5d42-a705-6818a09c4515" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279583 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=3.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279585 13194139534161 true] [17592186279584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279586 13194139534161 true] [17592186279584 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534161 true] [17592186279584 1041 "field-1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279584 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279586 13194139534161 true] [17592186279584 1110 #uuid "b3a9d6f6-b957-5ea4-80e1-212c6531bdb9" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279585 1034 "9" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279585 1111 #uuid "2a454b79-7424-5cc5-9a21-f341ebc41a83" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279586 1034 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279586 1111 #uuid "0cbece89-1b26-5184-9d69-8c70945c64c6" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279587 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279588 13194139534161 true] [17592186279587 1089 "q-342561" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279587 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534161 true] [17592186279587 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/adcee8bddcbbd96e18d63f85f5b9ebf2edc52897cc1ddcca7e377194462f6bda.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=3,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279587 1113 #uuid "7fd6ddaf-3c46-5030-b4a5-c0761f64013d" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279587 1145 "Since $B\\,(0,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279589 13194139534161 true] [17592186279588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279590 13194139534161 true] [17592186279588 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534161 true] [17592186279588 1041 "field-1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279588 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279590 13194139534161 true] [17592186279588 1110 #uuid "19491414-5359-58d0-962c-8d3a1746c460" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279589 1034 "3" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279589 1111 #uuid "285d5e04-6dab-56cc-8d98-a8430a74c834" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279590 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279590 1111 #uuid "d8d9e563-e33c-5037-b3ea-82ea794b6308" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279591 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279592 13194139534161 true] [17592186279591 1089 "q-342575" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279591 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534161 true] [17592186279591 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ce948d3eade308a90ffa85a7c7a88a8c403a6620ffda7e6d24444d2abac0770c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(1,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279591 1113 #uuid "337a9944-8b6d-5e9a-8280-a4fd810a5d34" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279591 1145 "Since $(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,4]$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279593 13194139534161 true] [17592186279592 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534161 true] [17592186279592 1041 "field-1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279592 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279593 13194139534161 true] [17592186279592 1110 #uuid "4cc73e86-36af-52ce-a446-19f7fb18768d" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279593 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279593 1111 #uuid "67da14c0-cb28-53af-8e70-84ccda2d27fe" 13194139534161 true] [17