({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279610 13194139534161 true] [17592186279609 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534161 true] [17592186279609 1041 "field-1" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279609 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279610 13194139534161 true] [17592186279609 1110 #uuid "12d98025-b7f2-5a95-9b83-f1927db5e6a3" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279610 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534161 true] [17592186279610 1111 #uuid "de19756b-6c9e-563b-9cb1-5a0b90131f73" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279611 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279612 13194139534161 true] [17592186279611 1089 "q-4962" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279611 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534161 true] [17592186279611 1100 "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ |" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279611 1113 #uuid "49f0cdee-0fae-592b-8a52-db3806d6bdc5" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279611 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(-2)=1$ and ${g}^{′}(1)=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534161 true] [17592186279612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279613 13194139534161 true] [17592186279612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279614 13194139534161 true] [17592186279612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279615 13194139534161 true] [17592186279612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279616 13194139534161 true] [1759218627